$CATEGORY: iso-iec-27004

::Note ahead of the following questions::
The following questions test whether a metric about the doing is told apart from
one about the effect, and how to notice that a measurement says nothing.
References are by standard, clause and edition.

// 1

::Measuring on its own::
Anyone who collects a number has a statement about the effect of the ISMS by
doing so.{FALSE
####False. ISO/IEC 27001\:2022 asks for four activities in 9.1. Without the
evaluating against a measure set beforehand it stays at numbers.}

// 2

::The clause that asks for the measurement::
Which clause of ISO/IEC 27001\:2022 asks for monitoring, measurement, analysis
and evaluation?{
=9.1 #Correct.
~9.2 #That is the internal audit.
~9.3 #That is the management review.
~10.1 #That is continual improvement.
####The other three numbers take the result in or follow from it. It is
collected in 9.1.}

// 3

::Doing or effect::
"Share of devices with encrypted storage". What does this metric measure?{
=The doing of a control. #Correct.
~The effect of the control on the risk. #That would need a measurement of what
actually happens on a loss.
~The information security objective. #The objective would be that a lost device
gives nothing up.
~The maturity of the management system. #A single share says nothing about that.
####It is cheap to collect and honest, as long as it claims no more. A hundred
per cent says nothing about whether a loss still does harm.}

// 4

::The levels of a metric::
Match what stands where.{
=At the bottom -> what is counted or read off directly
=In the middle -> what is calculated from that
=At the top -> the threshold and what happens when it is exceeded
=Beside it -> the objective the metric belongs to
####The practical value sits at the top. A metric with no threshold triggers
nothing and stops being collected after two quarters.}

// 5

::A number that can only rise::
A metric counts the people trained in total since the start. What is the
objection?{
=It cannot get worse and therefore measures nothing. #Correct.
~It is too easy to collect. #Effort is no measure.
~It belongs in the internal audit. #The audit asks about conformity, not about
the count.
~It needs an average. #An average changes nothing about the direction.
####A number that goes one way only can never fall below a threshold and
triggers nothing.}

// 6

::The wrong denominator::
A share is calculated against a device list in which six of eighty-six devices
are missing. How does that affect the number?{
=The share looks better than it is, because the missing devices do not count.
#Correct.
~The share looks worse, because devices are missing. #Missing devices are
missing from the denominator and not from the numerator.
~The share stays the same. #Only if the missing devices were in the same state,
and that is unknown.
~The share is invalid and is not reported. #It is reported, with the right
denominator.
####The population is the question asked least often in reports, and it gets the
most out.}

// 7

::The share with the right denominator::
78 of 86 devices are encrypted. What is the share in per cent, to one decimal
place?{#90.7:0.1
####With 80 as the denominator it would be 97.5 per cent. The same numerator and
a different denominator make a different statement.}

// 8

::What is read off three times::
The time from the reported loss to the withdrawal of access was 4, 31 and 96
hours in three cases. With a threshold of 24 hours, which two statements hold?{
~%50%The threshold was exceeded in two of three cases. #Correct.
~%50%The worst value is the statement and not the average. #Correct.
~%-100%The average of about 44 hours is the result. #An average covers up
exactly the case this is about.
~%-100%One case under the threshold shows the procedure holds. #One case shows
it can work, not that it does.
####With a threshold that holds for every case, the worst case counts. An
average answers a different question.}

// 9

::Gap, where the result goes::
The result of the measurement goes into the management review under ISO/IEC
27001\:2022, {=9.3 ~9.1 ~9.2 ~10.2}.{####9.1 is the measurement itself, 9.2 the
internal audit, and 10.2 the corrective action, which only follows once a
requirement has not been met.}

// 10

::Audit and measurement::
The internal audit under 9.2 replaces the measurement under 9.1, because both
check effectiveness.{FALSE
####False. The audit asks whether what was laid down is being done. The
measurement asks whether what was laid down works. Both results go into 9.3.}

// 11

::The fourth activity::
What is the activity from 9.1 called in which the result is read off a measure
set beforehand?{
=Evaluating
=evaluating
=Evaluation
####Without it there are numbers and no statement, and the measure has to stand
fixed before the measurement, or it gets set so the result fits.}

// 12

::A metric with no top::
The quarterly report says\: "Encryption rate of work phones\: 98 per cent.
Objective met." Write in two sentences what this metric is missing and which
second metric belongs beside it.{
####Model answer. It is missing the top level, meaning the threshold set
beforehand, the consequence when it is exceeded and who acts; besides that it
measures the doing and not the objective. Beside it belongs a metric about the
effect, for example the time from the reported loss to the withdrawal of access,
and that one can get worse.}
